        <div id="which-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Rooftop, Window or Portable: Which Camper AC Type Fits
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best air conditioner for a camper depends first on what you are cooling and where the power comes from. There are four realistic choices: a rooftop RV unit, a window unit in a small trailer, a plug-in portable AC, and a battery-powered portable. They differ by a factor of three in cooling and by more than that in cost per BTU, so getting the type right matters more than picking a brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rooftop Units</h3>

          <p>
            Rooftop units are the standard for travel trailers, fifth wheels and motorhomes. They deliver 13,500 to 15,000 BTU, mount in a 14 by 14 inch roof opening, take no floor or window space and are built for road vibration. If your rig has a roof opening and 30 or 50 amp service, a rooftop unit is almost always the right answer, and the Dometic Brisk II, Coleman Mach 15 and Furrion Chill are the mainstream choices.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Window Units</h3>

          <p>
            Window units are for small campers: teardrops, canned hams, tent trailers and truck campers without a rooftop unit. A 5,000 to 8,000 BTU rv window ac unit is cheap, efficient and draws little enough to run on a household outlet or a small generator. The catch is mounting, since most camper windows are not built for them, and they are not designed to travel installed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Portable and Battery ACs</h3>

          <p>
            Portable ACs, which plug into 120 volts and vent through a window, fill the gaps: cargo trailers, toy haulers, rentals and rigs whose rooftop unit cannot keep up. Battery portables like the EcoFlow WAVE 3 and Zero Breeze Mark 3 run without shore power at all, but at 5,000 to 6,000 BTU they suit small spaces only. They are the answer to a no-generator campground, not to a 30 foot trailer in Arizona.
          </p>

