        <div id="sizing-btu" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Many BTU Your Camper Needs
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Rule of Thumb for RVs</h3>

          <p>
            RVs need more cooling per square foot than houses. Walls are thin, windows are many, the roof sits in full sun and the door opens constantly. A common rule of thumb is one 13,500 BTU rooftop unit for a rig up to about 25 feet, a 15,000 BTU unit or two smaller units from 25 to about 32 feet, and two units, which usually means 50 amp service, above that. Dark exteriors, slide-outs and hot climates push you up a size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small Campers Flip the Problem</h3>

          <p>
            Small campers flip the problem. A 10 to 14 foot teardrop or canned ham has a tiny interior volume, and a 13,500 BTU rooftop unit cycles on and off so quickly that it cools without removing humidity, leaving the camper cold and clammy. A 5,000 to 8,000 BTU window or portable unit is a better match, and a 5,000 to 6,000 BTU battery unit can handle it after sunset.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shade and Insulation</h3>

          <p>
            Shade and insulation are worth real BTUs. Parking with the big windows away from the afternoon sun, using reflective covers on skylights and windshields, and running awnings can cut the heat load enough that a 13,500 BTU unit keeps up where it otherwise would not. A roof vent fan pulling hot air out in the evening helps the AC catch up faster.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">People and Cooking</h3>

          <p>
            Remember that the AC also has to keep up with people and cooking. Each adult adds roughly 300 to 400 BTU per hour, a family of four close to 1,500, and a stove or oven far more while it runs. If the AC barely holds the temperature empty, it will lose ground once everyone is inside for dinner. That is the main reason to size up, not down, in hot regions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="30a-vs-50a" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 30 Amp vs 50 Amp Service: What You Can Run
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Each Service Supplies</h3>

          <p>
            Your RV's electrical service sets a hard limit on air conditioning. A 30 amp RV connection is a single 120 volt leg, about 3,600 watts in total. A 50 amp RV connection carries two 120 volt legs at 50 amps each, about 12,000 watts. That difference is why rigs with two or three air conditioners are almost always 50 amp, and why a 30 amp trailer typically has just one rooftop unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Living Within 30 Amps</h3>

          <p>
            On 30 amp service, a 13,500 BTU rooftop unit running at about 1,500 watts leaves around 2,000 watts for everything else. That covers a fridge on electric, lights, a TV and charging. It does not cover a 1,000 watt microwave and a 1,400 watt electric water heater element at the same time as the AC. Most 30 amp tripped-breaker complaints come down to the microwave or a hair dryer running while the compressor kicks on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Household Outlets and Dogbone Adapters</h3>

          <p>
            Plugging into a household outlet with a dogbone adapter is even tighter. A typical 15 amp outlet supplies about 1,800 watts, and long, thin extension cords cause voltage drop that makes the compressor work harder and run hotter. A single 13,500 BTU unit can often run on a dedicated 20 amp outlet with a short, heavy cord, but it is marginal, and running anything else at the same time usually trips the breaker. A window or portable unit is the easier choice for driveway camping.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">50 Amp Service and Campground Voltage</h3>

          <p>
            On 50 amp service, you can run two rooftop units and normal appliances together, but only with good campground power. Low voltage on a hot afternoon at a crowded park is common, and it is hard on compressors. A surge protector with low-voltage cutoff protects the air conditioners, which are the most expensive appliances on most rigs, and is worth buying before your first summer trip.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Power Budget at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
