            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="soft-starters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Soft Starters: The Cheapest Upgrade for Any Rooftop AC
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Compressor Startup Surge</h3>

          <p>
            A conventional rooftop air conditioner has a single-speed compressor that slams on at full power. For a fraction of a second it draws a startup surge several times its running current, often called locked rotor amps. That surge is what trips 30 amp breakers when the microwave is on, what stalls small generators, and what overloads a power station's inverter even when it could easily handle the running load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How a Soft Starter Works</h3>

          <p>
            A soft starter is a small electronic module, installed inside the rooftop unit, that ramps the compressor up gradually and shrinks that surge dramatically. The Micro-Air EasyStart and SoftStartRV are the best-known brands. Installation takes an hour or two with basic tools, and many owners do it themselves following the maker's wiring guide; if you are not comfortable working with 120 volt wiring on a roof, have a technician do it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Payoff</h3>

          <p>
            The payoff is large. With a soft starter, many owners report running a 13,500 BTU rooftop unit on a single 2,200 watt inverter generator, which otherwise would not start it. It lets a 30 amp rig run the AC and the microwave together more often, and it is the difference between a power station starting the AC or shutting down with an overload error. It also reduces the electrical stress on the compressor at each start.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Does Not Change</h3>

          <p>
            A soft starter does not reduce running draw, though. A unit that pulls 1,500 watts once it is running still pulls 1,500 watts, and your generator or battery still has to supply that for hours. Think of it as the fix for startup, not for capacity. Pair it with properly sized power and it is one of the best-value upgrades you can make to any RV.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE UPGRADE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Install a soft starter before you buy a bigger generator.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A soft starter costs far less than stepping up a generator size, and it often lets a 2,200W inverter generator or a large power station start a 13,500 BTU rooftop unit that would otherwise trip it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="generator-sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Generator and Power Station Sizing for Camper AC
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sizing a Generator</h3>

          <p>
            For generators, size for the startup surge and running load together. Without a soft starter, a 13,500 BTU rooftop unit typically needs a 3,000 to 3,500 watt generator to start reliably alongside a few other loads. With a soft starter, many owners run it on a 2,200 watt inverter generator if little else is on. A 15,000 BTU unit, or two 13,500 BTU units on a 50 amp rig, pushes you toward 4,000 to 5,500 watts or larger. Our <a href="/best-rv-generator/">RV generator rankings</a> cover the best models in each class.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Altitude and Heat Derating</h3>

          <p>
            Altitude and heat reduce what a generator can deliver. A common derating rule is about 3.5% of output lost for every 1,000 feet above sea level, so a 3,000 watt generator at 7,000 feet behaves more like a 2,300 watt unit, exactly where soft starters and a slight capacity margin pay off. Inverter generators are quieter and give cleaner power, which suits modern RV electronics and campground quiet hours. Our <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">inverter generator guide</a> and <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> help with the math.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running a Rooftop AC on Batteries</h3>

          <p>
            Running a rooftop AC from batteries is possible but expensive. At 1,500 watts, a 5kWh battery bank at about 85% usable lasts under three hours of continuous running; in practice the compressor cycles, so you might stretch that to four or five in moderate heat. Whole-home-class units such as the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">large power stations we rank for home backup</a> can do it for an evening. Our explainer on whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-an-air-conditioner/">a portable power station can run an air conditioner</a> walks through the numbers for rooftop, window and portable units.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Battery and Solar Make Sense</h3>

          <p>
            Battery and solar make much more sense with smaller loads. A 5,000 BTU window unit at about 450 watts or a battery portable at 300 watts doubles or triples runtime from the same battery. If you camp off grid in a small camper, choosing a smaller, more efficient AC is usually smarter than buying a bigger battery. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best portable power stations for camping</a> list shows which units pair well with battery ACs, and <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">our power station sizing guide</a> helps match a battery to your loads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="window-and-portable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Camper Window Air Conditioners and Portables: Making Them Work
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealing the Opening</h3>

          <p>
            Window and portable units are where camper AC goes from bolt-in to improvised, and a little planning separates a setup that works from one that leaks and rattles. The first problem is the opening. Most RV windows slide horizontally or tilt out, while house window units are designed for vertical sashes. The usual fix is a framed opening in a wall, a dedicated through-wall sleeve or a custom panel that replaces one window pane.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Supporting the Weight</h3>

          <p>
            Support the weight properly. Even a small window unit weighs 35 to 50 pounds, and a camper wall is thin aluminum or fiberglass over light framing. An exterior bracket that carries the load to the frame, not the skin, prevents cracking. Seal around the unit with foam and weatherstripping to keep rain and insects out, and make sure it tilts slightly outward so condensate drains outside, not into the wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Venting a Portable</h3>

          <p>
            Portable units need the same attention to venting as they do in a house. Keep the hose short and straight, seal the window panel and choose a dual-hose or hose-in-hose design such as the Midea Duo where possible. A single-hose portable pulls cabin air outside and draws hot air in through every gap, which in a leaky camper can erase most of its cooling. Compare portables by their SACC rating, which is lower than the headline BTU on the box.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Securing Units for Travel</h3>

          <p>
            Travel is the other issue. Neither window nor portable units are built for road vibration. Remove a window unit before towing or support it so it cannot shift, and strap down a portable so it cannot tip in a hard stop. Drain any water from a portable's tank before moving, since sloshing water can reach the electronics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rooftop-buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Buying or Replacing a Rooftop RV Air Conditioner
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Standard 14 by 14 Opening</h3>

          <p>
            Most rooftop units mount in the standard 14 by 14 inch roof opening, which makes replacement easier than people expect. You buy the rooftop unit and, if the old ceiling assembly does not match, a new ceiling assembly or air distribution box. Ducted rigs need a unit compatible with ducted ceiling assemblies; non-ducted rigs use a ceiling box that blows air directly into the room. Check which you have before ordering.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Roof Load</h3>

          <p>
            Weigh the roof before you weigh the brands. Rooftop units run from about 75 to over 100 pounds. Your roof has a rated load, and the unit must sit on proper framing around the opening. Replacing a 13,500 BTU unit with a heavier 15,000 BTU heat pump model is usually fine, but confirm it, especially on older or lightweight trailers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat Pump Models</h3>

          <p>
            Heat pumps are worth considering if you camp in spring and fall. A heat pump version heats efficiently down to roughly freezing and saves propane on cool mornings. It will not replace a furnace in real cold. Low-profile units reduce height for garages and storage, at some cost in airflow. Quiet and variable-speed models are becoming more common; they cost more but are noticeably easier to sleep under.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Installation</h3>

          <p>
            Installation is a two-person job on the roof, with a ladder, a crane or a strong helper, sealing the gasket properly and connecting a 120 volt circuit. Plenty of owners do it themselves; if you are not comfortable on a roof or with wiring, a mobile RV tech can do a straight swap in a few hours. Ask them to fit a soft starter at the same time; it is cheaper while the shroud is already off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Safety and Maintenance
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Generator Safety</h3>

          <p>
            Generators are the main safety risk in camper air conditioning, not the AC itself. A generator running near an open window, under an awning or close to a neighbor's camper can push carbon monoxide inside. Keep it at least 20 feet from openings and pointed away, use a working CO alarm in the camper and test it before each trip. Never run a generator inside a toy hauler garage or an enclosed space.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electrical Protection</h3>

          <p>
            Electrical problems are the other risk. Low campground voltage, damaged cords and overloaded adapters overheat wiring and kill compressors. Use a surge protector with low-voltage cutoff, a cord rated for your service, and avoid chaining adapters. If a plug or cord feels hot, unplug and find out why before continuing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rooftop Unit Maintenance</h3>

          <p>
            Maintenance keeps rooftop units cooling well for years. Clean or replace the return air filters every few weeks during heavy use; a clogged filter can freeze the evaporator coil. Once a season, remove the shroud and clear leaves and debris from the condenser coil, check the roof gasket for leaks and straighten bent fins with a fin comb. Most performance complaints in the second or third summer come down to dirty coils.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery ACs and Power Stations</h3>

          <p>
            Battery ACs and power stations need less care, but do not leave them baking in a closed camper on a summer day when not in use, since heat ages lithium cells. Our guide to whether <a href="/are-portable-power-stations-safe-indoors/">portable power stations are safe indoors</a> covers sleeping-space precautions, and the same rules about clear vents and proper chargers apply to battery air conditioners.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size air conditioner do I need for my camper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As a rule of thumb, one 13,500 BTU rooftop unit covers rigs up to about 25 feet, a 15,000 BTU unit or two units suit 25 to 32 feet, and longer rigs need two units on 50 amp service. Teardrops and small trailers need only 5,000 to 8,000 BTU. Go up a size for hot climates and dark exteriors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run my RV air conditioner on a 15 amp household outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes, but it is marginal. A 15 amp outlet supplies about 1,800 watts, and a 13,500 BTU rooftop unit draws around 1,500 running, plus a large startup surge. A dedicated 20 amp outlet, a short heavy cord and a soft starter help. A window or portable unit is the more reliable choice for driveway camping.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator runs a camper air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Without a soft starter, plan on 3,000 to 3,500 watts for a 13,500 BTU rooftop unit with a few other loads. With a soft starter, many owners run one on a 2,200 watt inverter generator. Two rooftop units or a 15,000 BTU unit need 4,000 watts or more. Altitude reduces output by roughly 3.5% per 1,000 feet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a portable AC good for a camper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A good one can be, especially in toy haulers, cargo trailers or as a supplement to an undersized rooftop unit. Choose a dual-hose or hose-in-hose model, compare SACC ratings and plan for window adapting and condensate. In small travel trailers, portables take too much floor space to be a great primary unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a power station run an RV rooftop air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Large ones can for a few hours. A rooftop unit at about 1,500 watts empties a 5kWh battery in roughly three hours of continuous running, longer as the compressor cycles. You will usually need a soft starter for the surge. Smaller window or battery-portable units are far more practical when you want to run on batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a regular window air conditioner in a camper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with a proper mount. Most camper windows are not designed for window units, so owners build a framed opening or panel, support the weight with an exterior bracket and seal it against rain. Remove or secure the unit before towing, because house window units are not built for road vibration.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a soft starter and do I need one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A soft starter is a small module installed in the AC that ramps the compressor up gradually and cuts its startup surge sharply. You need one if you run the AC from a generator, a power station, a household outlet, or 30 amp service with other loads. It does not reduce running draw, only the startup spike.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Oversizing the AC in a small camper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 13,500 BTU rooftop unit in a 12 foot teardrop cools the air so fast it shuts off before removing humidity, leaving the camper cold and damp. Match capacity to the space: 5,000 to 8,000 BTU is plenty for most small campers and draws far less power.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the soft starter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without one, a rooftop unit's startup surge trips 30 amp breakers, stalls 2,200 watt generators and overloads power stations. A soft starter costs far less than a bigger generator and installs in a couple of hours. It is the single best upgrade for any conventional rooftop air conditioner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the microwave and AC on 30 amps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                30 amp service is about 3,600 watts total. A rooftop AC uses close to half of it, and a microwave or electric water heater can push you over. Turn the AC or water heater off while cooking, or use propane for the water heater, and the breaker stays on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a single-hose portable for an RV</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Single-hose portables blow cabin air outside and pull hot air in through every gap, and campers have plenty of gaps. Choose a dual-hose or hose-in-hose design, compare SACC ratings rather than the box BTU, and seal the window panel properly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring dirty coils and filters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A rooftop AC that cooled well last year and struggles this year usually has clogged filters or a dirty condenser coil. Clean filters every few weeks in heavy use and clear the condenser once a season. It costs nothing and restores most of the lost cooling.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

